<Project>

  <!--
    Conventions applied after each project's own content.

    NOTE: TargetFramework, SupportedOSPlatformVersion, TargetPlatformMinVersion and the
    TIZEN define are deliberately NOT set here - they live in eng/targets/TizenPackage.props.
    This file is imported at the end of Microsoft.Common.targets, long after the SDK has
    already parsed $(TargetFramework) into TargetFrameworkIdentifier/Version, so assigning
    the TFM here made inference fall back to identifier "_" and version "v0.0". See the
    comment in TizenPackage.props.

    The workload gate below stays a TARGET rather than an evaluation-time error, so that
    tooling which only evaluates projects (IDE load, `dotnet msbuild -getProperty`, the
    manifest validators) keeps working on machines without the workload. Actually building
    a Tizen project without the workload fails loudly.
  -->

  <!--
    Detects the Samsung Tizen workload.

    Runs at target time rather than during evaluation because item globs are the only
    layout-agnostic mechanism available, and they only work inside a target. See the
    Workload detection comment in Directory.Build.props for why the alternatives fail.

    Matches both known manifest layouts, but only within the current .NET feature line
    ($(TizenWorkloadBandPattern), e.g. 11.0.*):
      sdk-manifests/11.0.*/samsung.net.sdk.tizen/<version>/WorkloadManifest.json   (current)
      sdk-manifests/11.0.*/samsung.net.sdk.tizen/WorkloadManifest.json             (legacy)

    A Samsung workload installed under an older band (9.0.100, 10.0.100) does NOT satisfy
    net11 and must not lift the gate.

    Skipped entirely when TizenWorkloadAvailable was set explicitly, so CI and tests can
    pin the answer.
  -->
  <Target Name="_DetectTizenWorkload" Condition="'$(TizenWorkloadAvailable)' == ''">
    <ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(_TizenWorkloadManifestRoot)')">
      <_TizenWorkloadManifest Include="$(_TizenWorkloadManifestRoot)$(TizenWorkloadBandPattern)/samsung.net.sdk.tizen/*/WorkloadManifest.json" />
      <_TizenWorkloadManifest Include="$(_TizenWorkloadManifestRoot)$(TizenWorkloadBandPattern)/samsung.net.sdk.tizen/WorkloadManifest.json" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <PropertyGroup>
      <TizenWorkloadAvailable Condition="'@(_TizenWorkloadManifest)' != ''">true</TizenWorkloadAvailable>
      <TizenWorkloadAvailable Condition="'$(TizenWorkloadAvailable)' == ''">false</TizenWorkloadAvailable>
    </PropertyGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    Single source of truth for tooling that needs to know whether the workload is present.
    eng/build-workload-free.sh calls this instead of parsing `dotnet workload list`, so
    there is exactly one detection implementation to get right.

      dotnet msbuild <proj> -t:ReportTizenWorkload -nologo -v:m
  -->
  <Target Name="ReportTizenWorkload" DependsOnTargets="_DetectTizenWorkload">
    <Message Importance="High" Text="TizenWorkloadAvailable=$(TizenWorkloadAvailable)" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    The explicit external gate.

    The Samsung .NET 11 workload (Samsung.NET.Sdk.Tizen.Manifest-11.0.100-preview.7) is not
    published. Until it is, Tizen projects cannot build anywhere. This target says so
    plainly instead of letting the failure surface as NETSDK1013 ("The TargetFramework
    value 'net11.0-tizen11.0' was not recognized. It may be misspelled."), which sends
    people looking for a typo that isn't there.

    BeforeTargets deliberately lists the SDK's own pre-checks so that this error wins the
    race against whichever one fires first. Both are needed, and which one you hit depends
    on how well TFM inference worked:

      _CheckForUnsupportedTargetFramework       raises NETSDK1013 when $(TargetFramework)
                                                could not be parsed at all
      _CheckForUnsupportedTargetPlatformIdentifier  raises NETSDK1139 ("target platform
                                                identifier tizen was not recognized") once
                                                parsing DOES work but no workload provides
                                                the platform

    Before the TFM assignment was moved into props, inference failed outright and the
    symptom was NETSDK1013. Fixing that changed the symptom to NETSDK1139 and this gate
    silently stopped firing - so both are listed, and a regression test pins it.

    Restore and Build are also listed because the SDK pre-checks stop running once the
    workload ships, and this gate must still catch anyone whose workload is merely
    missing rather than nonexistent.

    Do NOT resolve a red build here by adding a neutral net11.0 fallback. A neutral build
    would be green and useless: it would produce assemblies that cannot run on Tizen,
    converting a visible external gate into an invisible correctness bug.
  -->
  <Target Name="ValidateTizenWorkloadAvailable"
          DependsOnTargets="_DetectTizenWorkload"
          BeforeTargets="_CheckForUnsupportedTargetFramework;_CheckForUnsupportedTargetPlatformIdentifier;Restore;Build;Pack"
          Condition="'$(IsTizenProject)' == 'true'">
    <Error Condition="'$(TizenWorkloadAvailable)' != 'true'"
           Code="MAUITIZEN0001"
           Text="The Samsung Tizen workload for .NET $(DotNetVersion) is not installed, so '$(MSBuildProjectName)' cannot target $(MauiTizenTargetFramework).%0A%0AThis is a known external gate, not a repository defect and not a typo in the target framework: no 11.0.100-band Samsung manifest ('Samsung.NET.Sdk.Tizen.Manifest-11.0.100-preview.7') has been published (only the 9.0.100 and 10.0.100 bands exist). See docs/migration.md and eng/baselines.json.%0A%0ATo build the parts of this repository that do not need the workload, run: eng/build-workload-free.sh%0ATo override detection once the workload ships, pass -p:TizenWorkloadAvailable=true." />
  </Target>


  <!--
    The neutral-fallback gate.

    The TFM assignment in eng/targets/TizenPackage.props is conditional, so ANY earlier source
    of a $(TargetFramework) value wins: an environment variable, a Directory.Build.props above
    the repository root, an IDE or tool passing -p:TargetFramework, or a copy-pasted project that
    sets it in its own body. Every one of those silently converts a Tizen project into a neutral
    one - and a neutral build is GREEN. It compiles, it packs, and it produces assemblies that
    cannot run on Tizen.

    That is precisely the failure this repository refuses to accept anywhere else (see the
    "Never fix a red build by adding a neutral fallback" note in Directory.Build.props), and it
    was the one place where it could happen by accident rather than by decision. It is a target
    rather than an evaluation-time error so that IDE load and property-only tooling keep working.

    MauiTizenAllowNonTizenTargetFramework exists for the deliberate case (a probe or verification
    lane that compiles Tizen sources against reference assemblies). Those projects set
    IsTizenProject=false instead, which is why nothing in this repository needs the escape hatch;
    it is there so the answer to "I really do mean this" is a visible opt-in rather than deleting
    the check.
  -->
  <Target Name="ValidateTizenTargetFramework"
          BeforeTargets="_CheckForUnsupportedTargetFramework;_CheckForUnsupportedTargetPlatformIdentifier;Restore;Build;Pack"
          Condition="'$(IsTizenProject)' == 'true' AND '$(MauiTizenAllowNonTizenTargetFramework)' != 'true' AND '$(TargetFramework)' != ''">
    <Error Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' != '$(MauiTizenTargetFramework)'"
           Code="MAUITIZEN0002"
           Text="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' is a Tizen project but its TargetFramework is '$(TargetFramework)' rather than '$(MauiTizenTargetFramework)'.%0A%0AA neutral or otherwise overridden target framework produces a build that succeeds and assemblies that cannot run on Tizen, so it is rejected rather than accepted silently. The override reached this project from an environment variable, a global property, an outer Directory.Build.props, or the project body itself.%0A%0AIf the project genuinely is not a Tizen artifact (a test host, a probe, a reference-assembly compile lane), set IsTizenProject=false. If you deliberately want a Tizen project on another target framework, set MauiTizenAllowNonTizenTargetFramework=true." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    README packing.
    
    Declared here, in .targets, rather than in Directory.Build.props, so that it is
    evaluated unambiguously after the project body has set $(IsPackable). It also works
    from .props - MSBuild evaluates all properties, including the project body, in an
    earlier pass than any item - but relying on that means the correctness of every
    shipping package rests on a multi-pass evaluation rule a reviewer has to recall.
    Two readers independently expected NU5039 from the .props placement, which is reason
    enough to move it: subtle-but-right is worse than obviously-right for something this
    cheap to relocate.

    eng/tests/PackReadmeProbe packs a project shaped exactly like a shipping package and
    the workload-free lane asserts README.md is inside the resulting nupkg, so this is
    pinned by behaviour rather than by argument.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(IsPackable)' == 'true'">
    <None Include="$(RepositoryRoot)README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
